          "idea_title": "Sacrificial Trapping Layers",
          "one_liner": "A temporary, shed-able biological layer on clothing that traps dirt and is then removed for a waterless cleaning cycle.",
          "concept_description": "This concept envisions a garment with a temporary, sacrificial outer layer made of a biological material like mycelium or a biofilm-derived polymer. This layer acts as a contaminant trap, adsorbing dirt and grime during wear, and is then shed or removed, taking the soil with it and leaving the underlying garment clean.",
          "how_it_works": "1. A very thin, flexible layer of mycelium (the root network of fungi) or a biopolymer like EPS is grown on or applied to the surface of a garment. 2. During wear, this sacrificial layer traps environmental particulates, oils, and other contaminants. 3. Instead of washing the garment, the user peels off or induces the shedding of the layer. 4. The shed layer, containing the trapped dirt, can be composted. A new layer could then be reapplied.",
          "synthesis_summary": "This new idea was created from a single, highly novel spark that proposes a 'sacrificial mycelial layer'. A new spark reinforced the concept with the more general 'sacrificial, easily detachable dirt-trap layers'. This is distinct from permanent bio-integrated textiles as it introduces a cycle of application, use, and removal. The synthesis focuses on this unique paradigm of a temporary, compostable 'skin' for the garment that serves as the primary interface for soiling, protecting the core textile and enabling a completely waterless cleaning method through shedding."
