        "original": {
          "idea_title": "Detachable Adsorbent Cleaning Elements",
          "one_liner": "Reusable cleaning pads made from highly absorbent materials like mycelium or activated charcoal that physically trap and remove dirt from clothing.",
          "concept_description": "This concept involves creating detachable pads or patches from highly absorbent natural or synthetic media. Unlike bio-active elements that degrade soil, these pads work by physically wicking and trapping oils, moisture, and particulate dirt from the fabric, which can then be removed.",
          "how_it_works": "1. Reusable or disposable pads are created from materials with high porosity and surface area, such as activated charcoal, cocopeat, algal biomass, or mycelium powders. 2. The pad is pressed against a soiled area of the garment. Minimal moisture may be used to aid the transfer of contaminants. 3. The absorbent material draws the soil into its matrix. 4. The pad is removed, taking the trapped dirt with it. Reusable versions could be cleaned separately.",
          "synthesis_summary": "This new idea was synthesized from sparks proposing a physical, rather than biological, cleaning mechanism for patches. One spark specified 'mycelium-derived cleaning patches' for absorption, while another suggested 'adsorbent natural media pads' like cocopeat and activated charcoal. A new spark added 'algal biosorbent powder' to the list of potential materials. The concept of a 'regenerable dirt-magnet' textile used as a cleaning tool and a 'mycelium-based dry-cleaning sponge' further strengthens the idea of using various materials for physical trapping and removal of soil."
        }
