          "idea_title": "Contaminant-Repellent and Easy-Release Surfaces",
          "one_liner": "A textile with a specially engineered surface chemistry or topography that repels dirt or releases it upon command, enabling easy, low-water cleaning.",
          "concept_description": "This concept involves engineering the surface of textile fibers to be ultra-smooth or to possess dynamic properties, preventing contaminants from adhering strongly. The goal is to create a fabric from which dirt, oils, and microbes can be easily removed with minimal physical force and water, or which can actively 'shed' contaminants when triggered.",
          "how_it_works": "1. Fibers are manufactured with superhydrophobic/oleophobic nanocoatings, biomimetic nano-textures, or specialized coatings (e.g., plasma-etched, hydrophilic, or biofilm-derived) making them highly repellent to soiling. 2. Alternatively, fibers are coated with a 'switchable' polymer (e.g., thermoresponsive, CO2-responsive) or feature dynamic microstructures that change conformation when triggered (e.g., by minimal moisture, temperature, or mechanical agitation), actively ejecting adhered particles. 3. As a result, cleaning requires only light rinsing or shaking to achieve a 'roll-off' effect.",
