          "overall_synthesis": "The concept for 'Electrostatic Particulate Trapping Textiles' was formed by merging two distinct but complementary principles from materials science and aerobiology. The primary inspiration came from an article on nanotechnology in textiles, which proposed fabrics with electrostatically charged fibers that attract particulates, analogous to an N95 mask. This established the core 'trapping' mechanism. This idea was reinforced and extended by a second source from aerobiology, which described using electrodynamic fields to trap and levitate aerosol particles for scientific study. This provided confidence in the 'release' mechanism, suggesting that electrostatic forces could be precisely modulated not just to attract, but also to control and eject particles, enabling a complete, waterless 'trap and release' cleaning cycle.",
