          "one_liner": "A cleaning system that uses vacuum pulses to drive minimal cleaning agents into fabric and then extract them with the dirt.",
          "concept_description": "This concept involves a cleaning system that uses vacuum pulses to first drive a minimal amount of cleaning agent deep into fabric fibers and then to extract the agent along with the dissolved soil. This active delivery and extraction method aims to provide a deep clean with very low liquid consumption.",
          "how_it_works": "1. A garment is placed in a chamber or a targeted cleaning head is applied to it. 2. The system applies a small amount of cleaning agent to the fabric surface. 3. A rapid vacuum pulse is initiated, lowering the pressure and drawing the cleaning agent deep into the yarn structure. 4. Subsequent pulses or a continuous vacuum then extract the liquid, carrying the dissolved contaminants with it. 5. The extracted liquid can be filtered and recycled.",
          "synthesis_summary": "This new idea was created from a single spark proposing 'vacuum-pulsed agent delivery'. This was identified as a unique process innovation for low-water cleaning. The synthesis focuses on the dual function of the vacuum: first to ensure deep penetration of a minimal amount of cleaner, and second to extract the soiled liquid. This active mechanical process distinguishes it from passive soaking or surface-level treatments."
