        "concept_description": "This concept outlines a compact, self-contained cleaning unit that uses a hybrid process to achieve high-level fabric sanitation with minimal water. By adapting principles from industrial wastewater treatment, it combines an Advanced Oxidation Process (AOP) to chemically destroy organic soils and a membrane filter to continuously purify and recycle a small volume of water within a closed loop. The result is a system capable of breaking down even recalcitrant contaminants without the high water and energy consumption of conventional washing, leveraging the proven effectiveness of hybrid purification systems [1].",
        "how_it_works": "1. **Application:** A treatment head applies a fine mist of water onto a targeted area of fabric. \n2. **Chemical Oxidation:** An integrated Advanced Oxidation Process (AOP)—potentially using UV light and a catalyst—is initiated within the mist. This generates highly reactive hydroxyl radicals that attack and decompose organic soil molecules directly on the fabric fibers, achieving significant contaminant degradation [1].\n3. **Extraction & Filtration:** The water mist, now containing the harmless breakdown byproducts, is suctioned from the fabric and passed through a high-efficiency membrane filter. \n4. **Purification & Recycling:** The membrane physically separates all remaining particulates, ensuring the water is purified to a high standard for immediate reuse [1]. The clean water is then returned to the reservoir to continue the closed-loop cleaning cycle.",
        "inspiration_summary": "The genesis of this concept was a direct analogical leap from the domain of large-scale industrial wastewater treatment. We observed how advanced facilities create powerful hybrid systems by pairing the chemical degradation of AOPs with the physical separation of membrane filtration to purify massive volumes of water [1]. The innovative question was: What if we miniaturized this industrial-scale, resource-intensive process into a compact, closed-loop device that cleans not a reservoir, but a single garment using a continuously recycled mist? This re-framing transforms a macro-technology for environmental remediation into a micro-technology for personal hygiene and resource conservation.",
