          "overall_synthesis": "The concept for 'Atmospheric Moisture-Harvesting Textiles' is derived directly from a single, powerful biological analogy. It translates the water-collection mechanism of the Namib desert beetle into a novel material science application. By observing how the beetle uses patterned wettability to survive in an arid environment, the idea re-frames the problem of water-scarce cleaning from one of efficiency (using less water) to one of self-sufficiency (sourcing its own water). The core innovation is embedding the water source directly into the fabric's structure, creating a garment that can passively clean itself.",
