        "concept_description": "This concept integrates specialized threads into bio-active textiles to create a simple, visual 'fuel gauge' for the garment's living system. By changing color, these threads provide a real-time, non-electronic signal indicating the health of the embedded microorganisms [1]. This solves a critical user-interface problem: how to know when a living garment requires care, ensuring the long-term efficacy and viability of its self-cleaning properties.",
        "how_it_works": "1. Threads coated with a pH-sensitive or other bio-indicator dye are woven into the textile alongside the active microbial colonies.\n2. During normal operation, the metabolic activity of the healthy microbes maintains a stable local chemical environment (e.g., a specific pH).\n3. When the microbial colony becomes stressed, dormant, or requires nutrients, its metabolic output shifts, causing a corresponding change in the local environment.\n4. This shift triggers a visible color change in the indicator dye on the threads, directly manifesting the microbes' well-being state and alerting the user that a rejuvenation cycle is needed [1].",
        "inspiration_summary": "The inspiration for this concept came from a human-computer interaction research paper that created a 'living artefact' to study how people interact with a system whose 'well-being' was communicated via color change [1]. In that study, a pH indicator visualized the metabolic activity of cyanobacteria. The analogical leap was to reframe this academic probe from a tool for exploring 'livingness' into a practical system management tool. We asked: 'What if this color-changing health monitor could serve as a fuel gauge for the microbes in a self-cleaning garment?' This transformed the concept into a crucial user interface for maintaining a complex biological system.",
